  "lessons": [
    {
      "title": "Say it shorter",
      "skill": "Loops",
      "body": "Solve Down the long dock with plain cards, then again with Repeat and End repeat, writing the number in Repeat's box. The score is cards: count every card between Start and End, Repeat and End repeat included.",
      "ask": "When does a Repeat save cards, and when does it cost one?"
    },
    {
      "title": "Find what repeats",
      "skill": "Patterns",
      "body": "Before laying cards for Round the pond, trace the path with a finger and say each move aloud. Circle the part you said again and again. That part goes between Repeat and End repeat.",
      "ask": "How did you decide where the loop starts?"
    },
    {
      "title": "Find the bug",
      "skill": "Debugging",
      "body": "Hand out the buggy row for Who moved the bread? Groups act it out, find the one wrong card, and fix the row with a single swap. Then do the same for Stones the wrong way, where the wrong card sits inside a loop.",
      "ask": "Why does one wrong card inside a loop go wrong more than once?"
    },
    {
      "title": "A loop inside a loop",
      "skill": "Nested loops",
      "body": "The big corner and Lap and hello need a Repeat inside a Repeat. Unroll one on paper: write out every card the duck does, in order, and count them. Then count the cards you laid.",
      "ask": "How many steps does the duck take for each card you laid?"
    },
    {
      "title": "Slow by the reeds",
      "skill": "Parameters",
      "body": "Speed and Look cards go under a card and change how it moves, not where. On the board they change nothing, and each one costs a card. Add Slow under one walk and Look left under another, and watch the score.",
      "ask": "If a card costs a point and does not move the duck, why would you lay it?"
    }
  ],
  "yours": []
}
